\subsection{Phase F: constructive localized stochastic stability}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-f}

The stochastic theorem in Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture} intentionally does
not infer a Gaussian drift inequality from deterministic bounded-input ISS.  A
samplewise converse metric $P_k$ is also not the right object for this step when
innovation acceptance is random, because a metric constructed from a complete
future switching sequence need not be measurable at the current filtration.
Phase F therefore lifts the proof to the finite contraction window of Phase B
and uses the ordinary Euclidean norm at those lifted indices.  The resulting
Lyapunov function is common to every admissible accepted/rejected branch and is
nonanticipative.

A second distinction is essential.  The Phase-C nonlinear certificate is
regional, whereas an exact Gaussian increment has unbounded support.  The proof
therefore first constructs a stochastic drift certificate for an explicitly
localized noise process whose increments remain inside the same robust tube,
and then bounds the probability that the true Gaussian process differs from
that localized process over a finite horizon.  No global Gaussian invariance is
claimed.

Fix a source mode of constant active dimension and the Phase-B window length
$N$ for which $\kappa_N<1$.  Let $k_n=k_0+nN$.  For a realized length-$N$
source branch $\beta_n$ (including accepted/rejected updates and any parameter
switching allowed by $\Pi^{\rm env}$), let
$\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}$ denote the zero-noise nonlinear lifted map obtained by
holding that same branch sequence fixed while setting the sensor/process
increments to zero.  Its linearization at the origin is
$\Psi_{n,\beta_n}$ and Phase B gives
\begin{equation}
 \|\Psi_{n,\beta_n}\|\le\kappa_N<1
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-map}
\end{equation}
for every admissible branch.  On a compact Phase-C tube of Euclidean radius
$r_{\mathcal T}$ define the finite lifted nonlinear coefficient
\begin{equation}
 L_N:=
 \sup_{n,\beta_n}
 \sup_{\substack{0<\|z\|\le r_{\mathcal T}\\
                  \text{all zero-noise prefixes remain in }\mathcal T}}
 \frac{\|\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}(z)-
          \Psi_{n,\beta_n}z\|}{\|z\|^2}<\infty.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-remainder}
\end{equation}
Each fixed source branch is smooth on the compact geodesic/measurement family,
so the bound is finite.  Define the deterministic lifted contraction
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \kappa_c:=\kappa_N+L_N r_{\mathcal T}<1.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-margin}
\end{equation}
Then, pointwise for every admissible branch,
\begin{equation}
 \|\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}(z)\|
 \le\kappa_c\|z\|.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-zero-noise}
\end{equation}
This condition is finite-window and nonanticipative; it is the stochastic
analogue of choosing a robust inner Phase-C tube.

Choose a physical-sample noise budget $w_*>0$ for which the Phase-C/E robust
invariance calculation verifies that every length-$N$ source prefix starting
in the selected inner set remains inside $\mathcal T$ whenever
$\|w_k\|\le w_*$ for every sample in the block.  Define the radial localization
\begin{equation}
 \vct w_k^{\,c}:=
 \begin{cases}
 \vct w_k,&\|\vct w_k\|\le w_*,\\
 w_*\vct w_k/\|\vct w_k\|,&\|\vct w_k\|>w_*.
 \end{cases}
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-noise}
\end{equation}
The localized and exact source trajectories are identical until the first raw
increment with norm exceeding $w_*$.  Localization may destroy conditional zero
mean, especially after NIS branch selection; the proof below does not use a
zero-mean cancellation.

For one physical sample on a fixed realized branch, write the difference
between the localized noisy and branch-matched zero-noise step as $y_k$.  Uniform
smoothness on the robust tube gives
\begin{equation}
 \|y_k\|
 \le \bar G\|w_k^c\|
 +c_{zw}r_{\mathcal T}\|w_k^c\|
 +c_{ww}\|w_k^c\|^2.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-one-step-noise}
\end{equation}
Assume the raw pre-gate increments obey the conditional moment bounds
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\|w_k\|^2\mid\mathscr F_k]\le s_2,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E[\|w_k\|^4\mid\mathscr F_k]\le s_4.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-moments}
\end{equation}
Because radial localization can only reduce the norm, the same inequalities
hold for $w_k^c$.  For conditionally Gaussian pre-gate noise with
$\Sigma_k\preceq\overline\Sigma$, one may choose
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 s_2=\operatorname{tr}\overline\Sigma,
 \qquad
 s_4=s_2^2+2\operatorname{tr}(\overline\Sigma^2).}
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-moments}
\end{equation}
The theorem therefore uses Gaussianity only to evaluate the two raw moments;
any other noise family with the same bounds is admissible.

Let $L_x<\infty$ be a uniform state-Lipschitz constant of one fixed-branch
source step on the robust tube.  Define
\begin{align}
 g_{\mathcal T}&:=\bar G+c_{zw}r_{\mathcal T},\\
 \nu_1&:=2g_{\mathcal T}^2s_2+2c_{ww}^2s_4,\\
 \boxed{
 \nu_N:=N\nu_1\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}L_x^{2r}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-floor}
\end{align}
No independence between the $N$ physical samples is required.  Indeed, if
$Y_n^c$ is the difference between the complete localized noisy lifted map and
its branch-matched zero-noise map, repeated Lipschitz propagation and
Cauchy--Schwarz give
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\|Y_n^c\|^2\mid\mathscr F_{k_n}]
 \le\nu_N.
 \label{eq:widen-lifted-noise-moment}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive lifted mean-square drift for localized gated noise]
\label{thm:widen-stochastic-drift}
Assume Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-margin}--
\eqref{eq:widen-lifted-noise-moment}.  Let
\begin{equation}
 V_n:=\|z_{k_n}^c\|^2,
 \qquad
 \boxed{
 \lambda_s:=\frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{2}<1,
 \qquad
 \sigma_s^2:=
 \frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{1-\kappa_c^2}\,\nu_N.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-coefficients}
\end{equation}
Then the localized exact source recursion satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E[V_{n+1}\mid\mathscr F_{k_n}]
 \le\lambda_s V_n+\sigma_s^2,}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}
\end{equation}
so Theorem~\ref{thm:capture-stochastic-live} applies to the localized regional
process with explicitly constructed $(\lambda_s,\sigma_s^2)$, and
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E V_n
 \le\lambda_s^{n-j}\mathbb E V_j
 +\frac{1-\lambda_s^{n-j}}{1-\lambda_s}\sigma_s^2.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the realized branch sequence write
$z_{k_{n+1}}^c=\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}(z_{k_n}^c)+Y_n^c$.  The branch itself may
be a function of the within-window localized noise; this causes no
measurability problem because Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-zero-noise} holds
pointwise for every admissible branch.  For any $0<\kappa_c<1$, choose
\begin{equation}
 \varepsilon=\frac{1-\kappa_c^2}{2\kappa_c^2}>0.
\end{equation}
The deterministic inequality
$\|a+b\|^2\le(1+\varepsilon)\|a\|^2+
(1+\varepsilon^{-1})\|b\|^2$, followed by
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-zero-noise} and
\eqref{eq:widen-lifted-noise-moment}, gives
\begin{align}
 \mathbb E[V_{n+1}\mid\mathscr F_{k_n}]
 &\le(1+\varepsilon)\kappa_c^2V_n
 +(1+\varepsilon^{-1})\nu_N\\
 &=\frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{2}V_n
 +\frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{1-\kappa_c^2}\nu_N,
\end{align}
which is Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}.  Iteration gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}.  The degenerate case $\kappa_c=0$ follows
by continuity or directly from the same inequality with any fixed
$\varepsilon>0$.
\end{proof}

For the true unbounded process define the raw-noise tail event over $K$ lifted
blocks,
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal E_{K}^c:=
 \left\{\max_{0\le r<K}\max_{0\le i<N}
 \|w_{k_{j+r}+i}\|>w_*\right\}.
\end{equation}
A union bound followed by Markov's inequality gives the distribution-free
fourth-moment estimate
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \Pr(\mathcal E_K^c)
 \le KN\min\!\left\{\frac{s_2}{w_*^2},
                      \frac{s_4}{w_*^4}\right\}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-exit}
\end{equation}
On the complementary event $\mathcal E_K$, every raw increment is unchanged by
localization, so the exact Gaussian/source trajectory and the localized
trajectory coincide over the entire horizon.  Therefore, with probability at
least one minus the right-hand side of Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-exit}, the
true process inherits the same regional trajectory and the same lifted
mean-square comparison pathwise up to that horizon.  A Gaussian or chi-square
tail can replace the moment bound when a sharper probability is desired.

\begin{remark}[What Phase F closes]
\label{rem:widen-stochastic-scope}
Phase F constructs stochastic drift coefficients from the finite deterministic
contraction $\kappa_N$, the lifted nonlinear coefficient $L_N$, compact
one-step Lipschitz/noise constants, and declared second/fourth moments.  The use
of a common $N$-step Euclidean contraction avoids an anticipative
switching-dependent Lyapunov metric and does not require a zero conditional
mean.  It does not turn a regional MEKF into a global Gaussian system, and it
does not assume that NIS gating preserves zero conditional mean.  For the true
unbounded Gaussian process the rigorous claim is finite-horizon and
high-probability unless additional global moment/recovery bounds are proved;
unbounded-noise excursions are not hidden inside a deterministic bounded-input
assumption.
\end{remark}
